To use the modular Firebase SDK (Version 9 and later) via Google's CDN (`gstatic`), you can load ES modules directly in your browser using `<script type="module">` tags[1]. 

Here is a complete HTML template demonstrating how to import and initialize Firebase App and Cloud Firestore from `gstatic`:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Firebase Firestore CDN Example</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1></h1>

    <!-- Import and use Firebase using ES modules from gstatic CDN -->
    <script type="module">
        // Import the functions you need from the respective SDKs on gstatic
        import { initializeApp } from 'https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/13.2.0/firebase-app.js';
        import { getFirestore, collection, getDocs } from 'https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/13.2.0/firebase-firestore.js';

        // Your web app's Firebase configuration
        const firebaseConfig = {
            apiKey: "YOUR_API_KEY",
            authDomain: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID.firebaseapp.com",
            projectId: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID",
            storageBucket: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID.appspot.com",
            messagingSenderId: "YOUR_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID",
            appId: "YOUR_APP_ID"
        };

        // Initialize Firebase
        const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);

        // Initialize Cloud Firestore and get a reference to the service
        const db = getFirestore(app);

        // Example: Fetching data from a collection
        async function getCities() {
            try {
                const querySnapshot = await getDocs(collection(db, "cities"));
                querySnapshot.forEach((doc) => {
                    console.log(`${doc.id} =>`, doc.data());
                });
            } catch (e) {
                console.error("Error getting documents: ", e);
            }
        }

        getCities();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Points to Remember:
1. **`type="module"`**: Crucial for browser environments when using ES module `import` syntax from URLs[1].
2. **Version Number**: Replace `13.2.0` in the URL path with the specific version of Firebase you want to target (or use the latest stable release)[1].
3. **Modular Functions**: Instead of namespaced syntax like `firebase.firestore()`, you explicitly import functions like `getFirestore()`, `collection()`, and `getDocs()`[2][3].

